          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A sequent calculus is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deductive+system"} deductive system
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
            (or something similar) in which the basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequent
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ⊢
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1, \phi_2, \ldots \vdash \psi_1, \psi_2, \ldots ,
          %p
            expressing the idea that some statement
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi_i
            must be true if every statement
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_i
            is true. The original sequent calculi were developed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Gentzen"} Gerhard Gentzen
            in 1933 building on anticipatory ideas by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Hertz"} Paul Hertz
          %p
            A central property of sequent calculi, which distinguishes them from systems of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            %a{:href => "#subformulaprop"} subformula property
            that it enjoys allows easy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/induction"} induction
            over proof-steps. The reason is roughly that, using the language of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/introduction+rule"} introduction rule
            %em either
            side of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent"} sequent
            with no
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elimination+rules"} elimination rules
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            We will start with an arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} signature
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+logic"} propositional logic
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            and unlimited variables for each type.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              We will be defining several terms below; but keep in mind that these are definitions
              = succeed "," do
                %em for sequent calculus
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              is a (finite)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list"} list
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mover
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{x}\colon \vec{T}
              or
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1\colon T_1, x_2\colon T_2, \ldots ,
            %p
              where the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
              and the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_i
              are (formulas for)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %p
            For the empty signature, the only context is empty; if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            consists of a single type, then a context is effectively a list of variables.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Given a context
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              %strong cedent
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              is simply a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list"} list
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{\phi}
              or
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1, \phi_2, \ldots
            %p
              of (formulas for)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
          %p
            (Recall that a proposition in the context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            allows the variables introduced by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong sequent
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              consists of a context
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              and two cedents in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antecedent"} antecedent
              and the
              = succeed "." do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/succedent"} succedent
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mover
                          %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mover
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{\phi} \vdash_{\vec{x}\colon \vec{T}} \vec{\psi} ,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mover
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{x}\colon \vec{T}
              is the context
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              has a single type (a so-called ‘untyped’ signature) and one tacitly assumes that the single type is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+type"} inhabited
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              has more than one type (all assumed inhabited), although more difficult if certain abuses of notation are used. Then the context can be ignored. This is not really the right way to do things, but (especially for untyped signatures) it is traditional.
            %p
              Confusingly, the cedents are sometimes also called ‘contexts’; then one speaks of the
              %strong left context
              (the antecedent), the
              %strong right context
              (the succedent). This is most easily done when ingoring what we have been calling the context; I'm not even sure what term would then be used for that context (maybe the
              = succeed "?)." do
                %strong type context
              %a{:href => "sequent#Gentzen2MartinLof"} interpretation of sequents as hypothetical judgements
              (which I hope to write soon), which shows how the type context, antecedent, and succedent are all used to form the context of a related
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypothetical+judgement"} hypothetical judgement
            %p
              One also sometimes uses ‘antecedent’ or ‘succedent’ for an
              %em individual
              proposition on the relevant side of the sequent; see also the use of ‘consequent’ in
              %a{:href => "#minimaletc"} minimal sequents
              below.
            %p
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              is an arbitrary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} set
              of sequents over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
              of the theory.
          %p
            We will explain below how the sequent calculus may be used to prove
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theorems"} theorems
            from the axioms; each theorem will also be a sequent.
          %p We may also consider particular kinds of sequents or theories, by form or content.
          #minimaletc.num_defn
            %h6#definitions_by_form Definitions (by form)
            %p
              A
              %strong closed sequent
              is one in which the context is empty; a
              %strong closed theory
              is one in which every sequent is closed.
            %p
              An
              %strong intuitionistic sequent
              is one in which the succedent consists of at most one proposition; an
              %strong intuitionistic theory
              is one in which every sequent is intuitionistic.
            %p
              A
              %strong minimal sequent
              is one in which the succedent consists of exactly one proposition (then called the
              = succeed ");" do
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/consequent"} consequent
              %strong minimal theory
              is one in which every sequent is minimal.
            %p
              A
              %strong dual-intuitionistic sequent
              is one in which the antecedent consists of at most one proposition; a
              %strong dual-intuitionistic theory
              is one in which every sequent is dual-intuitionistic.
            %p
              A
              %strong dual-minimal sequent
              is one in which the antecedent consists of exactly one proposition; a
              %strong dual-minimal theory
              is one in which every sequent is dual-minimal.
            %p
              A
              %strong classical sequent
              is simply a sequent, emphasising that we are not restricting to (dual)-intuitionistic or (dual)-minimal sequents; similarly, a
              %strong classical theory
              is any theory.
          %p
            These (except the first) are so-called because of their relationship to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/minimal+logic"} minimal logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logics"} logics
            may be presented using any sort of sequent, but Gentzen's original sequent calculi presented each logic using only corresponding sequents.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definitions_by_content Definitions (by content)
            %p
              A
              %strong regular sequent
              is one in which every proposition is given by a formula in
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+logic"} regular logic
              %strong regular theory
              is one in which every sequent is regular.
            %p
              A
              %strong coherent sequent
              is one in which every proposition is given by a formula in
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+logic"} coherent logic
              %strong coherent theory
              is one in which every sequent is coherent.
          %p Obviously, this list could be continued.
          %h2#rules_of_proof Rules of proof
          %p
            The basic building blocks of a proof in sequent calculus are
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rule+of+inference"} inference rules
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong rewrite rules
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mover
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\vec{\sigma}}{\tau}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{\sigma}
            is a (possibly empty)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/list"} list
            of sequents and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
            is a single sequent, to indicate that from the validity of the sequents in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{\sigma}
            that of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
            may be inferred, or that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{\sigma}
            may be
            %em rewritten
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deduction"} derivation
            or
            %strong proof tree
            is a compound of rewrite rules, such as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mn 1
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mn 2
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi σ
                            %mn 3
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mn 4
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mn 5
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\frac{\sigma_1 \;\; \sigma_2}{\sigma_3} \;\; \sigma_4 }{\sigma_5}
                  \\,.
          %p We will give the rules in several classes.
          .num_defn
            %h6#rules_structural_rules Rules (structural rules)
            %p
              The
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+rule"} identity rule
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mrow
                            %mover
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                            %mover
                              %mi T
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mi α
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{}{\alpha \vdash_{\vec{x}\colon \vec{T}} \alpha}
                    \\,,
            %p
              stating that, in any context
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              follows from itself, always.
            %p
              The
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substitution+rule"} substitution rule
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mrow
                            %mover
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                            %mover
                              %mi T
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mover
                          %mi ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mover
                          %mi s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mover
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mrow
                            %mover
                              %mi y
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                            %mover
                              %mi U
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mover
                          %mi s
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mover
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{
                    \\vec{\phi} \vdash_{\vec{x}\colon \vec{T}} \vec{\psi}
                    }{
                    \\vec{\phi}[\vec{s}/\vec{x}] \vdash_{\vec{y}\colon \vec{U}} \psi[\vec{s}/\vec{x}]
                    }
                    \\,,
            %p or
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi Γ
                        %mover
                          %mi ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi a
                          %mo *
                        %mover
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %msub
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi Δ
                        %msup
                          %mi a
                          %mo *
                        %mover
                          %mi ψ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\frac{
                    \\vec{\phi} \vdash_{\Gamma} \vec{\psi}
                    }{
